    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">How Did Travellers Reach Madeira Before the Airport?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>18 December 2026</span><span>6 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">By sea for centuries, then for a few years by flying boat. Until Madeira&#x27;s own airport opened on 8 July 1964, visitors arrived on ships that anchored in Funchal bay, from 1949 to 1958 on flying boats that landed on the water, and from 1960 by plane to Porto Santo and then on by boat.</p>

      <h2>How did people come by ship?</h2><p>On liners that called at Funchal on their way somewhere else. Madeira sat on the shipping routes from Britain to Africa and the Atlantic islands, so a stop here cost a ship very little. The Union-Castle mail ships from Southampton to South Africa listed Madeira as a port of call for decades, and a 1929 company brochure advertises sailings from Southampton every Friday via Madeira. The last Union-Castle mail ship sailed in 1977.</p><p>From Liverpool, the Yeoward Line ran weekly to Lisbon, Madeira and the Canary Islands from about 1899 until 1954, with the outward voyage taking eight to nine days. The Booth Line carried tourists to Lisbon and Madeira from 1903. For winter visitors, a week on a ship was simply part of the holiday.</p><h2>How did passengers get ashore without a deep-water quay?</h2><p>In small boats. Big ships anchored in the bay and passengers were rowed in. For a long time the only landing place was the shore of pebbles, about 2 km long, between the Pontinha rock at the west end of the bay and the São Tiago fort at the east. When the sea was too rough for the beach, people used steps cut into the Pontinha rock, carved in stone in 1850 or 1851 to replace a wooden stair.</p><p>Change came slowly, in this order:</p><ol><li><strong>1876 to 1878</strong>: a small quay is built at the Pontinha.</li><li><strong>1885 to 1890</strong>: a breakwater is built from the shore out to the islet, accepted by the state on 16 August 1890. A storm on 28 February 1892 wrecks much of it, and it is rebuilt by 1895.</li><li><strong>1939</strong>: the berthing quay is extended by 317 metres.</li><li><strong>1961</strong>: a further 457 metres are finished.</li><li><strong>18 July 1962</strong>: the port officially opens with room for ships to berth alongside.</li></ol><p>Ships also drew traders. The bomboteiros, a Madeiran word taken from the English "bumboat", were sellers who climbed aboard the steamers with photographs, postcards and island goods.</p><h2>When did the first plane reach Madeira?</h2><p>On 22 March 1921, and it landed on the sea. The aviators Gago Coutinho and Sacadura Cabral, with Ortins de Bettencourt and the mechanic Roger Soubiran, flew a Felixstowe F.3 flying boat from the river Tagus in Lisbon to Funchal bay in about seven and a half hours. According to the Portuguese Navy's museum, the point was to test navigation instruments. A year later Coutinho and Cabral flew across the South Atlantic to Brazil. The Madeira flight proved it could be done. It did not start a service.</p><h2>What were the flying boats to Funchal?</h2><p>The first regular air link, from 1949 to 1958. The British company Aquila Airways, founded in Southampton in 1948, flew its first trial to Madeira on 24 and 25 March 1949, refuelling on the Tagus at Lisbon on the way, and began weekly flights on 14 May 1949. Its aircraft were big four-engined flying boats: Short Hythes, converted from wartime Sunderlands, and later Short Solents.</p><p>They landed in the bay and stayed at anchor offshore. A boat called the Aquila acted as a floating control post, and another, the Fernanda, carried passengers and luggage to the dock at the Pontinha. Winston Churchill flew home this way after his Madeira holiday in January 1950.</p><p>The era ended badly. On 15 November 1957 an Aquila Solent crashed into Chessell Down on the Isle of Wight soon after taking off, killing 45 of the 58 people on board. Aquila's last flight, Funchal to Southampton via Lisbon, was on 30 September 1958. A Portuguese company, ARTOP, took over the Lisbon to Funchal route the next day with two Martin Mariner flying boats named Madeira and Porto Santo. On 9 November 1958 the Porto Santo disappeared over the Atlantic with 36 people aboard. No wreckage was ever found, and ARTOP stopped flying two days later.</p><h2>Why did Porto Santo get an airport first?</h2><p>Because it had the space. Porto Santo is low and much flatter than Madeira, with room for a long runway. Its airport saw a first landing, a TAP DC-4, on 20 July 1960, and opened officially on 28 August 1960. For four years the quickest way to Funchal was to fly to Porto Santo and finish the journey by boat, a crossing that still takes about two to two and a half hours today on the ferry Lobo Marinho.</p><h2>When did Madeira's own airport open?</h2><p>On 8 July 1964, when a TAP Super Constellation from Lisbon landed at Santa Catarina, near Santa Cruz, at 11:24. The first runway was only 1,600 metres long. It was stretched to 1,800 metres in 1986, and in 2000 to 2,781 metres, partly on a platform carried by 180 columns about 70 metres tall over the shore. Since 29 March 2017 the airport has carried the name of Cristiano Ronaldo. For arriving today, see our <a href="/posts/getting-to-madeira-guide">guide to getting to Madeira</a>.</p><h2>Can you still arrive in Madeira by sea?</h2><p>Only on a cruise ship, and that is the honest answer for most people. Funchal's port handled about 750,000 cruise passengers and 331 ship calls in 2025, according to the port authority's figures. A car and passenger ferry from Portimão on the mainland ran from 2008 to about 2012, with a crossing of roughly 24 hours, and came back for the summers of 2018 and 2019 only. There is none today, although in July 2026 the national parliament recommended setting one up again.</p><h2>What can you still see of that history from the water?</h2><p>Most of the stage. The bay where the liners anchored and the flying boats came down is the same water a boat crosses leaving Marina do Funchal, and the Pontinha, where passengers were landed, stretches out on the west side of it. Our article on <a href="/posts/what-is-pontinha-funchal">the Pontinha and its fort</a> tells that part of the story.</p><p>Every Chifbay trip begins in that bay, and on the way back you approach Funchal as the old arrivals did, from the sea, with the whole amphitheatre of the city opening up behind the harbour wall. Chifbay's <a href="/private-boat-tour-madeira">private boat tour in Madeira</a> page explains the trips, and the <a href="/practical">practical page</a> shows where on the seafront to find the marina.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>When did Madeira airport open?</h3><p>On 8 July 1964, when a TAP Super Constellation from Lisbon landed at Santa Catarina, near Santa Cruz.</p><h3>Did seaplanes fly to Madeira?</h3><p>Yes. Aquila Airways ran flying boats from Southampton via Lisbon to Funchal bay from 1949 to 1958, and ARTOP flew Lisbon to Funchal briefly in late 1958.</p><h3>Is there a ferry from mainland Portugal to Madeira?</h3><p>Not at the moment. A ferry from Portimão ran from 2008 to about 2012 and again in the summers of 2018 and 2019.</p><h3>Who made the first flight to Madeira?</h3><p>Gago Coutinho and Sacadura Cabral, with two crew, in a Felixstowe F.3 flying boat from Lisbon to Funchal bay on 22 March 1921.</p>

      <blockquote>For most of its history, Madeira&#x27;s arrivals hall was the bay itself.</blockquote>
    </article>
